namespace eval Examples {
    variable _ruff_ns_opts {
        -heading {Examples}
    }
    variable _ruff_preamble {
        This section contains examples of usage of interpolation/approximation procedures of the package.
        List of availible examples:
        - [1D Linear and nearest interpolation] - "examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl" file
        
        ## 1D Linear and nearest interpolation

        The two most basic types of interpolation are linear and nearest ones. These types are availible with procedures
        [::tclinterp::interpolation::lin1d] and [::tclinterp::interpolation::near1d].
        To demonstrate both type we use shifted sinusoidal function:
        #ruffopt excludedformats nroff
        ```math
        f(x) = \sin(10x)+1.1
        ```
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}

        #ruffopt includedformats nroff
        ```
        f(x) = sin(10 ⋅ x) + 1.1
        ```
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}

        Relative error is calculated here and in all subsequent chapters is defined as:
        #ruffopt excludedformats nroff
        ```math
        \text{Rel.err.}=\frac{f_{\text{exact}}-f_{\text{interp}}}{f_{\text{exact}}}\cdot 100\%
        ```
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}

        #ruffopt includedformats nroff
        ```
                   f      - f             
                    exact    interp       
        Rel.err. = ──────────────── ⋅ 100%
                        f                 
                         exact            
        ```
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}


        Import all procedures from `::tclinterp` namespace:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region import-packages

        Define procedures for sinusoidal function calculation, error calculation and for generating sequence of x
        values:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region create-procedures

        Create input data, interpolation points `xi` and exact values at these points:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region generate-input-data

        Input data has points with 0.05 step, `xi` values are separated by 0.01 step.

        Zip input data for `ticklecharts` plotting:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region zip-input-data

        Call interpolation function [::tclinterp::interpolation::lin1d] that returns list of interpolated y values, and
        zip `xi` and `yInterpLin` points:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region linear-interpolation

        Do the same for [::tclinterp::interpolation::near1d]:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region nearest-interpolation

        Zip exact values:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region zip-exact-data

        Calculate errors:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region calculate-errors

        Plot and save results using `ticklecharts`:
        @include-code examples/linear_near_interpolation.tcl -region plot-ticklecharts

        #ruffopt excludedformats {nroff}
        Results are:

        !ticklechart_mark_linear_near_interpolation!
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}
    }

}
